  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 111 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite\u00a0imagery shows the upper level low near the \nFour Corners at this time. It is still forecast to track northeast\nacross Colorado, but models favor a slightly\u00a0more northerly track\nthan earlier advertised. We nudged precipitation amounts down \nslightly because of this track shift.\u00a0Easterly low level flow \ncontinues to produce widespread low clouds and drizzle east to the\nContinental Divide. This easterly flow is a downslope component \nwest of the divide\u00a0and satellite imagery\u00a0currently shows mostly \nsunny skies at this time. As the upper level low nears, ridge top \nwinds turn more southerly, which should cut off the downslope \nflow. The next batch of lift and precipitation is over south \ncentral Colorado and will lift northeast, bringing rain and \nmountain snow late this afternoon and evening.\u00a0 Then overnight as \nthe low shifts over northeast Colorado, northwest flow (more \ntypical) is expected to lead\u00a0to orographic lift in addition to the\ndeformation zone. Meanwhile, eastern Colorado is expected to see \na lull in precipitation, except for continued drizzle. The \ndeformation zone shifts east over northeast Colorado bringing rain\nshowers. The heaviest rainfall amounts and best chance for rain \nwill be over the northern part of the state. If the more northerly\nlow trend continues, areas along and south of I-70 may see very \nlittle additional precipitation Thursday. Rain and mountain snow \nwill end Thursday afternoon as the upper level low weakens and \nmoves east of the area.\u00a0\n\nThe remnants of the upper level low will be northeast of the \nState by Thursday evening. Much drier air will move into the area \nleading to clearing skies during the evening. This will lead to \ngood radiational cooling and a chilly overnight period Thursday \nnight/Friday morning. Most locations are expected to see a frost \nor hard freeze therefore, we have issued a freeze watch for \nThursday night and into Friday morning. We maintain a northwest \nflow aloft on Friday as a ridge develops to our west and a trough \nto our east. Surface high pressure over Colorado will lead to a \nsunny day with light winds and cool temperatures for Friday. Highs\nwill be slightly below normal with readings in the mid to upper \n50s.\u00a0\u00a0\n\nThe ridge to the west moves over Colorado Saturday and we see\nridging continue through Monday. A weak trough passes through the\nridge on Sunday. There's enough moisture for clouds, but not\nenough for precipitation. So we may see a slight dip in\ntemperatures Sunday with the increase in cloud cover. Highs are\nexpected to be in the 70s Saturday through Monday, with some upper\n60s possible Sunday if cloud cover persists for much of the day.\nSouthwest flow aloft is expected for Tuesday and Wednesday as a\nstrengthening trough along the west coast pushes the ridge east of\nthe region.\u00a0Temperatures will continue to run above normal under\nthis pattern with dry conditions persisting. This trough along the\nwest coast is the next system to watch for precipitation\nacross\u00a0our area late next week.",
